      "question_id": "argument_consequence_against_compatibilism",
      "category": "argument_reconstruction",
      "question": "Reconstruct van Inwagen's consequence argument and explain the strongest\ncompatibilist response to it.\n",
      "retrieved": [
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "skepticism-moral-responsibility",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/",
          "score": 0.56998026,
          "snippet": "Hard incompatibilism posits that human beings lack basic desert moral responsibility regardless of whether the universe is deterministic or indeterministic. This claim is supported by the 'Luck Pincer' argument, which demonstrates how various forms of luck undermine the control necessary for moral accountability in both libertarian and compatibilist frameworks."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "leibniz-modal",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/leibniz-modal/",
          "score": 0.5652804,
          "snippet": "Leibniz argues that each individual substance possesses a complete concept containing all its predicates, which determines its existence in one specific possible world among an infinity of compossibles. God selects the actual world as the best of these possibilities based on divine goodness and wisdom, thereby reconciling metaphysical necessity with human freedom through compatibilism."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "hume-freewill",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume-freewill/",
          "score": 0.5574268,
          "snippet": "Strawson argues that both classical compatibilists and libertarians err by over-intellectualizing the nature of moral responsibility. He distinguishes between exemptions, where an individual is deemed unsuitable for reactive attitudes, and justifications that may require amending those attitudes."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "creation-conservation",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/creation-conservation/",
          "score": 0.5498788,
          "snippet": "Most physicalists accept van Inwagen's assumption regarding the causal requirement, even though rejecting this constraint would facilitate finding a solution to the puzzle. This widespread adherence suggests that the causal requirement possesses significant intuitive appeal."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "incompatibilism-arguments",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-arguments/",
          "score": 0.5484238,
          "snippet": "The text examines arguments asserting that free will is incompatible with determinism, distinguishing between compatibilist and incompatibilist positions while introducing the concept of impossibilism. It analyzes specific logical structures such as van Inwagen's Consequence Argument and critiques Lewis's counterfactual responses to these claims. The discussion also evaluates various intuition-based arguments and clarifies definitional shifts in contemporary philosophical literature regarding moral responsibility."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "incompatibilism-arguments",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-arguments/",
          "score": 0.54755014,
          "snippet": "The passages analyze the philosophical debate between compatibilism and incompatibilism regarding free will, determinism, and moral responsibility. Key arguments include van Inwagen's Consequence Argument, Frankfurt-style cases challenging alternative possibilities, and Strawson's defense attorney argument, while also addressing critiques of Lewis and evaluations of probabilistic causation by Robert Kane. The discussion further explores manipulation scenarios involving Ernie and Bert to test ultimate origination and examines methodological approaches such as interventionist causation theories."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "compatibilism",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/compatibilism/",
          "score": 0.54549414,
          "snippet": "Compatibilism argues that free will is compatible with determinism by challenging incompatibilist arguments, particularly the Consequence Argument and claims regarding alternative possibilities. Key figures such as John Martin Fischer and Paul Russell advance strategies like reasons-responsiveness or Strawsonian approaches to redefine control without requiring the ability to do otherwise."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "compatibilism",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/compatibilism/",
          "score": 0.54133517,
          "snippet": "Compatibilism asserts that free will and moral responsibility are compatible with determinism by redefining control through mechanisms such as reasons-responsiveness, hierarchical models of desire, and Strawsonian reactive attitudes. This position challenges incompatibilist arguments regarding alternative possibilities and the Consequence Argument by focusing on an agent's internal dispositions and guidance control rather than the ability to do otherwise. The framework has evolved from classical empiricist accounts to modern revisions incorporating dispositionalist theories."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "incompatibilism-arguments",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-arguments/",
          "score": 0.047388062,
          "snippet": "incompatibilism-arguments  Incompatibilists think that this, and any compatibilist account of the ‘can’ of freedom of choice, is, and must be, mistaken. The Consequence Argument (Ginet 1966, 1983, 1990; van Inwagen 1975, 1983, 2000; Wiggins 1973; Lamb 1977) is widely regarded as the best argument for this conclusion. In the remainder of this section we will take a closer look at van Inwagen’s version of this important and influential argument."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "compatibilism",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/compatibilism/",
          "score": 0.04170496,
          "snippet": "compatibilism  It is fair to say that the Consequence Argument earned the incompatibilists an important dialectical advantage. Compatibilists owe us an account of what’s was wrong with the Consequence Argument, and perhaps also, some positive account of the ability to do otherwise. So even though many compatibilists are committed to thinking that the Consequence Argument is unsound, it nevertheless set the agenda for many contemporary compatibilist theories of free will and moral responsibility."
        }
      ],
