          "score": 0.573723,
          "snippet": "The passages argue that knowledge of metaphysical modality is acquired through diverse epistemic methods, including empirical investigation, counterfactual reasoning, and rational intuition, rather than relying on a single source. These approaches address fundamental questions about how humans access modal truths and justify claims regarding necessity and possibility across different philosophical frameworks. Key debates center on the roles of conceivability, essence, and imagination in grounding our understanding of what is possible or necessary."
        },
        {
