          "score": 0.56734055,
          "snippet": "The passages advance the thesis that metaphysical truths and facts are grounded in or supervene upon a base of perfectly natural properties. This framework utilizes concepts such as duplication, indiscernibility, and grounding completeness to establish how qualitative similarities across possible worlds determine metaphysical necessity. The argument further explores syntactic regimentation via higher-order logic and addresses minimality conditions for these fundamental properties."
        },
        {
