      "question_id": "comparative_locke_reid_personal_identity",
      "category": "comparative",
      "question": "Reconstruct Locke's memory criterion of personal identity and explain\nReid's brave-officer objection. What does the objection show about the\ntransitivity of identity?\n",
      "retrieved": [
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "reid-memory-identity",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reid-memory-identity/",
          "score": 0.69165295,
          "snippet": "Thomas Reid argues that David Hume's theory of memory erroneously replicates the flaws found in John Locke’s account. This critique is famously illustrated through the case of the Brave Officer, which challenges their respective views on personal identity and continuity."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "reid-memory-identity",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reid-memory-identity/",
          "score": 0.6669016,
          "snippet": "Thomas Reid argues against the 'theory of ideas' and Locke's Memory Theory by positing that memory is a direct, immediate faculty involving a conception of a past event accompanied by a belief in its occurrence. He contends that personal identity requires numerical sameness grounded in a continuous self rather than fleeting memories, which serve only as first-personal evidence for this identity."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "identity-ethics",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-ethics/",
          "score": 0.6592889,
          "snippet": "The text examines critiques of Locke's psychological criterion for personal identity, specifically addressing objections from Butler and Reid regarding transitivity and the presupposition of memory. It explores alternative frameworks such as biological organism theories, nonreductionism, and four-dimensionalist reductionism to resolve paradoxes like fission cases. Ultimately, it argues that while traditional identity relations face logical challenges, moral responsibility may be grounded in psychological continuity rather than strict numerical identity."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "reid",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reid/",
          "score": 0.6249394,
          "snippet": "Thomas Reid argues that conception is the fundamental intellectual faculty providing intentional content, directly opposing the 'Way of Ideas' and Hume's representationalism which he views as epistemologically flawed. He further develops a direct theory of memory where past events are immediate objects, thereby refuting Locke’s psychological continuity account and establishing personal identity on first principles rather than circular reasoning."
        },
        {
