          "title": "hermeneutics",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hermeneutics/",
          "score": 0.676651,
          "snippet": "Heidegger reorients hermeneutics from Husserl's epistemological foundations to fundamental ontological inquiries into human existence, arguing that the phenomenological epoché is inadequate for capturing factual lived experience. He posits that self-interpretation is inherently difficult due to worldly involvement, social averaging, and historical tradition, a view later expanded by Paul Ricoeur through his analysis of language and narrative."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "sep",
          "title": "husserl",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/husserl/",
          "score": 0.6360999,
          "snippet": "Husserl’s phenomenology analyzes intentional consciousness by distinguishing between propositional meanings, indexical contents anchored to a determinable X, and the noematic quality of experiences. Through methods like epoché and reduction, he investigates how perceptual singularity and time-consciousness structure experience while bracketing existence assumptions. Furthermore, intersubjective empathy is posited as fundamental for constituting both the objective self-image and the shared spatio-temporal world."
        },
        {
